Eco-xPD: Enhancing deep learning based Parkinson’s disease detection with domain adaptive knowledge distillation from high-cost imaging to low-cost modalities
Farah Binta Haque, Md. Shoaib Akhter Rafi , Md. Kamrul Hasan ∗
Department of Electrical and Electronic Engineering, Bangladesh University of Engineering and Technology (BUET), Dhaka, 1205, Bangladesh
article info
Keywords:
Parkinson’s disease Deep learning Dual-domain representation learning Domain adaptation Implicit relational knowledge infusion Raw-transform domain fusion
abstract
Early and reliable detection of Parkinson’s disease (PD) is increasingly important given the rising global prevalence of the disorder and the limited accessibility of high-cost diagnostic imaging. Although neuroimaging techniques such as DaTscan provide high diagnostic accuracy, their expense and limited availability restrict widespread use. This work introduces a clinically informed multimodal deep learning framework that transfers neurodegenerative knowledge from high-fidelity DaTscan imaging to low-cost, non-clinical biomarkers, including voice recordings, smartwatch sensor signals, or hand-drawing patterns while training, and discarded during inference. A central contribution of this study is cross-modal relational domain adaptation (xReDA), a cross-modal adaptation framework designed specifically for heterogeneous modalities. Unlike conventional domain adaptation approaches that operate at the instance level and typically require source and target data to share similar input-output structures, xReDA aligns how the imaging network learns, i.e., its relational and structural learning behavior, rather than what it learns. By distilling relational neurodegeneration patterns from DaTscan-derived feature graphs into non-clinical encoders, xReDA enables cross-domain knowledge transfer that is difficult to achieve effectively using traditional adaptation methods. For each non-clinical modality, the framework employs two complementary representation pathways: a raw domain encoder (RDE) that learns spatial-temporal patterns from raw signals or drawings, and a transformed domain encoder (TDE) that captures spectral and dynamic signatures associated with PD progression. Their outputs are integrated through a dual-stream fusion attention block (DFAB), which adaptively models inter-modal dependencies, prioritizes informative feature source, and produces a unified multimodal embedding with enhanced discriminative capacity. Extensive evaluations across six publicly available non-clinical PD datasets demonstrate that the proposed framework achieves upto 8.33% and 21.89% improvements in accuracy and F1-score, respectively, along with up to 5.93% gain in early disease detection compared to existing methods. Incorporating DaTscan-guided relational supervision through xReDA further enhances model stability, highlighting the value of cross-modal knowledge transfer in PD detection. Overall, this study establishes a practical framework for PD screening by combining clinical insight with accessible non-clinical biomarkers. The proposed approach offers a scalable, low-cost, and reliable alternative to imaging-based diagnostics, paving the way for practical early detection systems deployable in diverse healthcare environments.
