has garnered considerable attention owing to its accessibility and high diagnostic sensitivity. Approximately 90% of individuals with PD exhibit hypokinetic dysarthria, characterized by diminished pitch variation, breathiness, and articulatory instability, which may manifest years before overt motor symptoms [15,16]. While DL models trained on raw voice waveforms outperform traditional handcrafted feature-based approaches by capturing spectral-temporal dependencies [17], frequency-domain representations offer more discriminative and physiologically meaningful features. Accordingly, mel spectrogram-based CNNs have demonstrated superior diagnostic accuracy compared with raw-signal-based approaches [18,19]; however, custom CNN architectures may still underperform pretrained deep networks due to their limited representational capacity and generalization ability. Smartwatch and accelerometer data have emerged as a promising modality for continuously monitoring tremor, bradykinesia, and gait disturbances associated with Parkinson’s disease [13,20]. Deep learning models applied to multi-axial accelerometer and gyroscope signals effectively discern complex spatiotemporal motor patterns without manual feature extraction [21]. Beyond the temporal domain, frequency domain characteristics, such as tremor spectral energy, harmonic distortion, and rhythmic irregularities, are highly indicative of PD-induced motor dysfunction [22]; however, the potential of pretrained architectures for the diagnosis of motion-based PD remains largely unexplored. Hand-drawn image analysis offers a cost-effective means of quantifying fine motor control through digitized spiral or line-drawing tasks, with tremor-induced oscillations, trajectory deviations, and reduced smoothness serving as diagnostic biomarkers [14,23]. Two-dimensional representations preserve spatial dependencies and movement dynamics more comprehensively than one-dimensional transforms [14], and pretrained CNNs generally exhibit stronger generalization than custom architectures [24]. Notably, transformed-domain features remain underexplored in this modality, despite evidence that DWT-based representations yield significant improvements over raw hand-drawing inputs [25]. On the other hand, despite the promising achievements across these low-cost modalities, most extant research efforts remain confined to iso
lated domains, either raw data [14,21,26] or frequency domain representations [19,25], thereby overlooking the inherent complementarity between them. Consequently, there remains a notable gap in the literature regarding frameworks for PD detection that can jointly leverage time-domain signals and their corresponding frequency-domain characteristics in a cohesive and computationally efficient manner. The lack of such unified modeling strategies limits the ability of existing approaches to fully capture complementary spatiotemporal cues that are critical for a more robust PD detection performance. Although few recent studies have focused on leveraging multiple data modalities rather than a single modality for PD detection [13,27–29], such multimodal prediction systems, despite their potential performance gains, typically require multiple sensing devices or recording modules. This substantially increases system complexity and reduces accessibility, particularly for at-home screening scenarios. Moreover, the performance improvements achieved by multimodal frameworks are often marginal when weighed against their added architectural complexity, increased inference time, and the operational burden of deploying multiple sensing devices. Existing state-of-the art multimodal frameworks are mentioned in Table 1 along with their input modalities, datasets, number of subjects, and performance. Recent advances in multimodal learning have progressed beyond explicit feature concatenation toward more flexible knowledge transfer paradigms, including self-supervised contrastive representation learning, co-attention mechanisms, and latent contrastive alignment [36, 37]. Conventional knowledge distillation methods [38,39] have focused mainly on transferring knowledge from large teacher networks to lightweight student models in paired-input settings, where both networks process identical samples and direct feature-level correspondence can be established. Consequently, these approaches inherently rely on instance-level alignment and paired feature imitation. Despite substantial progress, most existing multimodal frameworks still assume the availability of paired data across modalities, limiting their applicability in a realistic healthcare environment characterized by heterogeneous datasets, acquisition constraints, and incomplete modality overlap. Bridging this gap requires a framework capable of transferring diagnostically informative knowledge from clinically reliable modalities to cost-effective unimodal systems such that the auxiliary clinical modality is required only during training and can be discarded during inference. However, this objective remains highly challenging because realworld datasets rarely contain paired samples between expensive clinical modalities and accessible personal-device modalities for the same subjects, rendering conventional distillation strategies ineffective under inter-subject variability and severe cross-modality domain discrepancies [40,41]. Unlike traditional distillation frameworks, the primary objective of this study is not to compress the knowledge of a large network into a smaller one, but rather to transfer clinically informative knowledge from a neuroimaging-driven model into non-clinical, low-cost sensing modalities-driven network under completely unpaired conditions. Specifically, the proposed framework aims to distill disease-relevant structural knowledge learned from DaTscan neuroimaging data into networks trained on personal-device signals such as voice, accelerometer, gyroscope, and handwriting data. To the best of our knowledge, no prior work has explored knowledge transfer from clinical neuroimaging modalities to heterogeneous non-clinical signals in such an unpaired setting. Inspired by relational knowledge distillation (RKD) [42], the proposed approach reformulates knowledge transfer at the structural level by encouraging the student network to preserve the relational geometry of the neuroimaging embedding space through pairwise distance and angular relationships, rather than directly mimicking individual feature activations. Building upon this formulation, the proposed cross-modal relational domain adaptation (xReDA) framework jointly integrates relational distillation with adversarial latent-space alignment. The adversarial discriminator reduces modality-specific discrepancies by bringing the em
