(i) A cross-modal relational domain adaptation framework is proposed that transfers clinically enriched knowledge from DaTscan neuroimaging to low-cost personal-device signals (i.e., voice, smartwatch inertial data, or hand-drawn images) without requiring DaTscan at inference. This framework facilitates the effective integration of diagnostic information from multimodal clinical imaging with accessible unimodal deployment, supporting the scalable and clinically grounded evaluation of PD. (ii) Modality-aware dual-domain pipelines are designed for each lowcost signal, combining raw and transformed-domain representations through a proposed raw domain encoder (RDE), transformed domain encoder (TDE), and dual-stream fusion attention block (DFAB). Each modality, including voice, smartwatch, and hand-drawn image signals, is encoded using domain-specific representations such as mel spectrograms, inertial time-frequency features and filtered trajectory-based descriptors, respectively, resulting in enriched PDrelevant feature representations compared to single-domain baselines. (iii) A pretrained DaTscan-based neuroimaging encoder is employed as a privileged clinical feature extractor, leveraging its capacity to capture dopamine transporter abnormalities. Its latent representations serve as a clinically informed reference signal, guiding the training of non-clinical encoders without requiring paired imaging-signal data at inference time.
(iv) A geometry-aware relational adaptation strategy, xReDA, is introduced for unpaired cross-modality knowledge transfer. Rather than direct feature imitation, xReDA transfers the structural organization of the neuroimaging latent space by modeling angular relationships and pairwise distances between embeddings. Combined with an adversarial distributional alignment module for reducing the modality gap between neuroimaging and non-clinical modalities, this yields a more consistent latent representation space that preserves clinically relevant structural information, extending standard adversarial and distillation-based approaches in a complementary manner. (v) Rigorous evaluation across six independent datasets spanning voice, smartwatch, and hand-drawing modalities demonstrates the effectiveness, robustness, and cross-signal versatility of the proposed framework, providing empirical evidence supporting the effectiveness of transferring neuroimaging-informed relational knowledge into low-cost sensing modalities for PD screening.
2. Dataset
The summary of all datasets used in this study is shown in Table 2. The table additionally reports the distribution of subjects across different PD stages, categorized as early, moderate, and advanced. Specifically, early-stage PD is defined based on UPDRS-II [43] levels 0 and 1; level 2 corresponds to moderate PD; and levels 3 and 4 are considered advanced PD. For the MDVR-KCL dataset, staging is determined using the Hoehn and Yahr Rating (HYR) scale [44], following analogous thresholding to maintain consistency across datasets. Our analysis indicates that the majority of subjects, particularly in the voice-based datasets, belong to the early-to-mid stages of PD, which aligns with the study’s emphasis on early disease detection. Since speech impairment is often among the earliest observable symptoms of PD, these datasets provide a clinically relevant benchmark for evaluating low-cost non-clinical modalities. To further improve clinical coverage across different disease severities, the MDVR-KCL dataset was incorporated due to its explicit stage annotations spanning early, moderate, and advanced PD. In addition, the DaTscan dataset also contains disease severity information, enabling the neuroimaging teacher network to learn stage-dependent pathological characteristics. Through the proposed distillation framework, these clinically informative representations, particularly those associated with early-stage PD, can be transferred to the non-clinical student networks.
