2.2. Voice data
Four publicly available voice datasets are used in this study, each capturing distinct speech characteristics relevant to PD detection. The datasets utilized for voice to PD detection task are as follows:
(i) YouTubePD [2] is the first publicly available multimodal benchmark for PD analysis, comprising over 200 in-the-wild YouTube videos with visual recordings, facial landmark annotations, and audio data. Audio tracks were extracted from interview videos featuring public figures, yielding 58 PD and 201 HC clips of approximately 10 s each, designed to capture speech manifestations of PD such as reduced vocal intensity, monotonicity, and tremor. (ii) Cross-YouTubePD was constructed by merging YouTubePD with the PD patients’ speech dataset (PPSD) [45], a collection of clinically diagnosed PD speech recordings developed by the SJTU-Yongfu Research Group. Since YouTubePD was predominantly HC, additional PD recordings from PPSD were incorporated to balance the dataset (68 PD, 201 HC). (iii) VoicePD [46] comprises 81 sustained vowel phonation recordings (53 PD, 28 HC), along with participant metadata including age, sex, and health status. (iv) Mobile Device Voice Recordings at King’s College London (MDVRKCL) dataset [31] was collected at King’s College London Hospital using a Motorola Moto G4 smartphone in a realistic phone-call setting. During the recording procedure, participants (16 PD, 21 HC) performed scripted reading tasks, followed by spontaneous conversational speech to capture diverse vocal characteristics.
2.3. Smartwatch dataset
The PADS dataset [47] includes movement data from 469 participants (PD patients, differential diagnoses, and HC) recorded via wristworn smartwatches and a smartphone across 11 neurologist-designed tasks, yielding 5159 measurement steps. Sensor signals (acceleration and rotation) are provided alongside demographic and clinical metadata.
2.4. Hand-drawn image dataset
The HandPD dataset [48] comprises handwritten spiral and meander tasks from 92 participants (74 PD, 18 HC), collected at Botucatu Medical School, São Paulo State University, Brazil. The dataset yields 736 cropped JPG images (368 spirals, 368 meanders). The HC group consists of 6 males and 12 females (ages 19–79), and the PD group of 59 males and 15 females (ages 38–78).
3. Methodology
3.1. Proposed cross-modal relational domain adaptation
The proposed cross-modal relational domain adaptation (xReDA) framework (see Fig. 1) is developed to support optimal cross-domain knowledge transfer for cost-effective PD detection. The framework adopts a unified learning strategy with adaptive weighting, in which a non-clinical data-driven network learns the structural characteristics of a neuroimaging-based network to enable knowledge transfer while preserving stable diagnostic performance. Specifically, xReDA consists of three principal components: (1) relational angular knowledge distillation (RAKD), which preserves the angular relationships among feature embeddings; (2) relational distance knowledge distillation (RDKD), which preserves the pairwise distance relationships within the embedding space; and (3) adversarial learning-based domain adaptation (ALDA), which aligns the latent feature distributions of neuroimaging and non-clinical modalities through adversarial training. Collectively, these components form a geometry-aware and modality-agnostic learning framework that jointly preserves relational structure while reducing cross-domain distribution discrepancies, enabling effective unpaired knowledge transfer. Within the xReDA architecture, the RAKD and RDKD modules operate collaboratively to transfer fine-grained relational knowledge from the neuroimaging domain to the non-clinical domain. To employ these modules, the outputs of both encoders are passed through additional fully connected networks (FCNs) with linear activation, which can be formulated as
