3.2. Architecture of the neuroimaging network
A DaTscan is one of the most accurate clinical tools for detecting PD. It visualizes dopaminergic neuron activity using the radioactive tracer 123I-Ioflupane, which binds to dopamine transporters in the striatum. In HC, normal DaT binding produces a comma- or crescent-shaped uptake pattern in both hemispheres, whereas in PD, reduced dopamine transporter availability, particularly in the posterior putamen, results in an asymmetric or dot-shaped appearance (see Fig. 2) [49]. The extent and symmetry of signal loss correlate with disease severity. Since DaTscan provides the most definitive visualization for differentiating PD from HC in comparison to other imaging modalities, e.g., MRI, or CTscan [50], it has been utilized as the neuroimaging model in this study. The illustrated architecture in Fig. 3 represents a DenseNet121-based CNN used as the neuroimaging supervisor network for PD detection from DaTscan images. The network processes DaTscan inputs of size 224 × 224 × 3, extracting progressively abstract features through a sequence of dense blocks and transition layers. As shown in the figure, the backbone consists of an initial convolutional layer followed by four dense blocks, each separated by a convolution-pooling transition layer that reduces spatial dimensionality while preserving feature reuse. Within each Dense Block, every BN-ReLU-Conv layer receives concatenated feature maps from all preceding layers, enabling
rich multi-scale gradient flow and alleviating vanishing gradient issues, a property particularly beneficial for the subtle and spatially distributed imaging biomarkers characteristic of PD in DaTscan data. The final dense block’s output is passed through a global average pooling (GAP) layer, yielding a compact 1024 × 1 feature vector. In parallel, an adapter sub-network processes the 1024 × 1 GAP features through a bottleneck projection (1024 → 512 → 1024), providing a domain-regularized auxiliary representation. The fused features are then projected through a series of fully connected layers (256 × 1) and reduced to a 2 × 1 output, followed by a sigmoid activation (σ) to produce the final binary PD prediction ŷt. The classification process can be illustrated as follows:
