3.3.1. Voice-net architecture
Voice impairments arise from motor control deficits affecting phonation, articulation, and respiration for PD patients. Acoustically, PD speech exhibits increased jitter and shimmer (unstable pitch and amplitude), reduced harmonic-to-noise ratio (breathy or rough voice), diminished pitch and intensity variability (monotone speech), and slower, irregular rhythm. Overall, speech rate and energy dynamics decline, with longer pauses and reduced prosody [53]. These anomalies manifest as blurred harmonic structures, increased high-frequency noise, flattened formant trajectories, and lower spectral variance over time in the mel spectrogram domain. PD spectrograms often appear less vibrant, with weak energy in higher bands [54]. Arbitrary samples of both PD and HC voice recordings are shown in Fig. 5, where the mel spectrogram for PD (see Fig. 5(d)) shows weaker harmonic structures with reduced high frequency energy compared to HC (see Fig. 5(b)). For the voice modality, a dedicated network, Voice-Net (see Fig. 6), is designed to effectively capture discriminative acoustic representations associated with PD. Each voice recording is segmented into short overlapping intervals of 1.5 s at 44.1 kHz to ensure sufficient temporal resolution while maintaining spectral consistency. For each segment, two parallel representations are generated: (i) the raw voice waveform Xr ∈ R1000×1, and (ii) its corresponding mel spectrogram Xf ∈ R250×250×3, computed using a short-time Fourier transform (STFT), followed by mel filter bank processing. Formally, the mel spectrogram can be expressed as
