3.5. Implementation details
The hardware specifications for the experimental setup used in this work are provided in Table 3. Detailed training and testing procedures are presented in the following subsections.
3.5.1. Training details
For each modality, both the non-clinical and neuroimaging datasets are partitioned into training, validation, and testing subsets following a 70 ∶ 15 ∶ 15 split ratio to ensure fair evaluation and avoid data leakage. The training process is conducted in two sequential stages: (i) independent pretraining of the neuroimaging network as well as the nonclinical network, and (ii) supervised adaptation of the corresponding non-clinical network under the guidance of the pretrained neuroimaging network. During the pretraining phase, both the neuroimaging network and the non-clinical networks are trained independently to learn modalityspecific representations. The Adam optimizer is employed with an initial learning rate of 1 × 10−4. To ensure stable convergence, a ReduceLROnPlateau strategy is applied, reducing the learning rate when validation performance plateaus for 10 consecutive epochs. Pretraining is conducted for a maximum of 100 epochs, with an early stopping criterion that halts training if no improvement is observed for consecutive 35 epochs. After pretraining, the non-clinical network is trained under the supervision of the neuroimaging model to facilitate knowledge transfer through the proposed xReDA mechanism. The Adam optimizer is again utilized with an initial learning rate of 1 × 10−4, following the same learning rate scheduling and early stopping criteria as in the pre-training phase. The total training is conducted for up to 300 epochs. During this phase, the adapter module and the three FCN layers of the neuroimaging network, utilized for knowledge transfer, are also fine-tuned with a smaller learning rate of 1 × 10−5 to allow adaptive alignment with the evolving feature space of the non-clinical network, while keeping the base DenseNet121 network frozen.
3.5.2. Testing details
During inference, each non-clinical network is evaluated exclusively on the test set corresponding to its sensing modality. At this stage, the non-clinical network operates in a fully autonomous manner, completely detached from the neuroimaging network, thereby demonstrating its ability to generalize and make predictions without any external guidance or modality-level supervision. Owing to our innovative xReDAbased distillation strategy, the non-clinical network internalizes and adopts the structural learning behavior of the neuroimaging network, enabling it to extract more discriminative representations and ultimately achieve improved performance. This setup ensures that the reported performance reflects the intrinsic representational power learned by each non-clinical model, independent of direct supervision.
