4. Result analysis
Table 4 presents the comparative performance of PD prediction across multiple modalities: voice, smartwatch, and hand-drawing, evaluated against state-of-the-art approaches, both with and without the proposed xReDA module. The performance metrics are obtained by running the each method on five different random seeds and getting the average and standard deviation (SD) of them. The results clearly demonstrate the consistent superiority of the proposed framework in all modalities and datasets, with particularly remarkable gains in generalization and discriminative precision once xReDA is incorporated. On the YouTubePD dataset, prior voice-based approaches exhibit limited discriminative capability. Shen et al. employ a multi-kernel CNN-RNN architecture, Islam et al. utilize an attention-augmented CNN, and Lv et al. rely on a VGGish-based feature extractor, achieving F1scores of 58.76%±7.59%, 60.51%±1.15%, and 57.25%±6.86%, respectively. He et al. extract handcrafted acoustic representations using the Geneva Minimalistic Acoustic Parameter Set (GeMAPS) and the INTERSPEECH 2009 Emotion Challenge (IS09) feature set, followed by prediction through a learning-based classifier, yielding an F1-score of 60.68%±1.48%. While such handcrafted-feature-driven strategies offer interpretability, they remain sensitive to speaker variability and domain shifts. Siniukov et al. [59] suggested a HuBERT based network with domain adversarial neural network (DANN)-based adaptation, achieving 60.79%±4.28% F1-score. The proposed framework without xReDA already improves performance on YouTubePD, achieving an F1-score of 70.47%±9.42% through joint modeling of raw waveform and frequency-domain representations with dual-stream fusion. When xReDA is incorporated, performance increases substantially to an F1score of 82.68%±2.92%, corresponding to approximately 12% performance gain. Notably, the standard deviation is significantly reduced from 9.42% to 2.92%, indicating improved training stability and more consistent performance across folds. This improvement indicates that cross-modal relational supervision effectively transfers discriminative cues from auxiliary DaTscan-derived representations, enhancing robustness against intra-speaker variability. To further evaluate cross-domain generalization, models are tested on Cross-YouTubePD dataset. Siniukov et al. showed the worst generalization with F1-score 61.83%±1.02%. Shen et al. and Islam et al. achieve F1-scores of 65.52%±2.88% and 63.15%±1.17%, respectively. Lv et al. and He et al. demonstrate relatively stronger generalization with F1-scores of 67.17%±1.32% and 68.09%±3.09%. The proposed model without xReDA maintains a competitive F1-score of 76.77%±3.22%, highlighting the effectiveness of its unified representation learning. With xReDA enabled, the framework further improves to an F1-score of 85.68%±2.31%, outperforming all competing methods by a clear margin. A further reduction in standard deviation (from 3.22% to
2.31%) highlights enhanced generalization stability under domain shift conditions. Finally, on the VoicePD dataset, the proposed framework achieves 99.30%±0.51% F1-score without xReDA and reaches almost perfect classification performance with 99.91%±0.06% F1-score when supervised with xReDA, surpassing both handcrafted-feature-based approaches and deep attention-driven architectures. The near-zero SD (i.e., 0.06%) further reflects highly reliable and consistent predictions across different splits. Collectively, these results demonstrate that xReDA not only improves in-domain accuracy but also plays a critical role in enhancing cross-dataset generalization in voice-based PD prediction. On the MDVR-KCL dataset, which contains clinically annotated PD severity stages spanning early, moderate, and advanced conditions, the proposed framework also demonstrates substantial improvements over existing voice-based approaches. Shen et al. achieve an F1-score of 92.27%±0.83%, while Islam et al., Lv et al., and He et al. report F1-scores of 91.36%±0.45%, 90.68%±1.47%, and 90.25%±1.65%, respectively. The HuBERT-DANN-based framework proposed by Siniukov et al. achieves comparatively lower performance with an F1-score of 88.95%±0.52%. In contrast, the proposed framework without xReDA already attains a significantly higher F1-score of 96.51%±0.23%, demonstrating the effectiveness of the proposed dual-stream representation learning framework in capturing disease-relevant speech characteristics across varying PD severities. When xReDA is incorporated, the performance further improves to an F1-score of 98.16%±0.15%, outperforming all competing methods by a considerable margin. Furthermore, the extremely low standard deviation highlights highly stable optimization and robust generalization across folds. Since the MDVR-KCL dataset provides explicit disease severity annotations, we further evaluate model performance across different PD severity levels (see Fig. 11). The average results on each severity level demonstrate that the proposed method consistently outperforms existing approaches across all stages, including early, moderate, and advanced PD. Notably, the performance gains introduced by xReDA are more pronounced in the early and moderate stages, where discriminative features are typically more subtle. This observation further validates the effectiveness of the proposed distillation strategy in capturing fine-grained, clinically relevant patterns. The comparatively lower performance observed in the advanced severity stage may be attributed to the limited availability of samples within that category. For the PADS dataset, existing smartwatch-based methods, such as Varghese et al.’s feed-forward neural network, Hur et al.’s hybrid LSTM-GRU architecture, and Jovanovic et al.’s CNN enhanced with metaheuristic optimization achieve F1-scores in the range of 87–090%, primarily constrained by their limited ability to capture long-range motion dependencies and contextual movement patterns. The proposed approach surpasses all competing methods, attaining an F1-score of 92.56%±1.17% without xReDA through the use of stacked threedimensional spectral representations and a multi-channel ConvLSTM network that jointly models spatial-temporal motion dynamics. With xReDA enabled, performance further improves to 93.60%±1.03%, exceeding the closest competitor by approximately 3%. A modest yet consistent reduction in standard deviation (from 1.17% to 1.03%) further indicates improved robustness and reduced sensitivity to subject-specific variations. This gain can be attributed to xReDA’s ability to align smartwatch motion embeddings with relational priors derived from DaTscan representations, thereby reducing domain-induced variability and improving robustness to subject-specific behavioral differences. Additionally, for the hand-drawn image modality, methods in Wang et al. (LSTM + CNN), Ali et al. (Chi-squared aware network), and Kansizoglou et al. (residual architecture) reach 92–95% F1-scores. However, the proposed system achieves 96.59%±0.85% F1-score with xReDA by integrating cross-modal relational learning, allowing effective transfer of neuro-motor relational patterns between drawing and DaT domains. The relatively low standard deviation (i.e., 0.85%) further supports the stability and reliability of the proposed approach in this modality.
