5. Discussion
5.1. Discussion on unpaired cross-modal, cross-subject knowledge distillation
The primary objective of this study is fundamentally different from that of conventional knowledge distillation (KD). Traditional KD is primarily designed to transfer knowledge from a computationally heavy teacher network to a smaller student network, where the emphasis is placed on model compression, computational efficiency, and preserving predictive consistency between paired teacher-student samples [63]. In contrast, the proposed framework focuses on transferring clinically informative knowledge from a diagnostically reliable modality to accessible non-clinical modalities. Consequently, the teacher network in this study is not required to be architecturally larger or computationally heavier; instead, its importance arises from the richness and clinical reliability of the modality from which it learns. Furthermore, unlike standard KD settings, the teacher and student data in this framework are neither paired nor modality-aligned, making conventional instancelevel distillation strategies ineffective.
Standard KD assumes paired inputs, where teacher and student networks process corresponding samples, enabling direct feature-level, logit-level, or relational alignment [42]. However, in the proposed crossdomain setting, the neuroimaging modality (DaTscan) and the nonclinical modalities (e.g., voice, hand-drawn images, smartwatch signals) originate from different subjects and heterogeneous sensing domains [64–66]. As a result, instance-level correspondence is unavailable, and samples within a batch are not guaranteed to be semantically aligned. Although RKD partially alleviates this limitation by transferring structural relationships rather than individual embeddings [42], conventional RKD formulations still implicitly assume that teacher and student representations originate from paired or distributionally compatible data [67]. This assumption significantly limits the applicability of classical RKD in fully unpaired cross-modal scenarios. From the conventional understanding of RKD, introducing classconstrained relational alignment may initially appear theoretically desirable. Restricting relational transfer to semantically consistent groups (e.g., PD–PD and HC–HC relationships) intuitively seems likely to reduce optimization ambiguity and strengthen semantic consistency between teacher and student spaces. Such formulations are generally effective in homogeneous or modality-consistent settings, where class-conditioned manifolds are expected to share comparable geometric topology. Under those conditions, preserving class-specific relational structure can reinforce discriminative alignment and improve representation consistency. However, the proposed framework operates in a fundamentally different setting, where the latent organization of disease classes is not expected to remain geometrically consistent across modalities. Each non-clinical modality captures Parkinsonian characteristics through fundamentally different physiological mechanisms and feature representations: voice encodes dysphonia and hypokinetic dysarthria characteristics [64], smartwatch signals capture gait dynamics and motor fluctuations [65], and hand-drawn images reflect micrographia and tremor-related irregularities [66]. In contrast, the DaTscan teacher network organizes its embedding space according to neuroimaging-derived biomarkers associated with striatal dopamine transporter availability. Consequently, the geometric organization of PD and HC samples in the neuroimaging space is not expected to be isomorphic to the organization induced by any individual behavioral modality. Under these conditions, enforcing strict class-conditioned relational preservation introduces a substantially stronger structural constraint
