 to imitate individual teacher embeddings; instead, it gradually reorganizes its embedding manifold so that its global geometric structure becomes compatible with that of the clinically informative neuroimaging network while remaining adaptive to the intrinsic properties of the target modality. Two additional mechanisms further stabilize this process. First, the supervised classification loss preserves disease-discriminative separation throughout training, preventing RKD from driving the student toward degenerate geometric configurations. The combined effect becomes a form of label-preserving geometric regularization, where classification anchors semantic separability while RKD promotes globally coherent relational structure. Second, adversarial domain confusion enforces approximate alignment between teacher and student embedding distributions prior to relational comparison [69], reducing modalityspecific structural discrepancies before RKD is applied. This distinction is important because the relational geometries being compared already
originate from partially aligned latent spaces rather than arbitrarily mismatched distributions. To further reduce the representation gap, the framework additionally performs partial fine-tuning of the teacher’s final refinement layers during training, while keeping the DaTscan backbone frozen, allowing the teacher representation to become more compatible with the student embedding domain without sacrificing clinically grounded feature organization. To further analyze this behavior, t-SNE visualizations of the teacher representations (see Fig. 15) reveal that raw embeddings from the penultimate layer exhibit clear class separability, whereas the corresponding difference vectors significantly reduce this separation. This suggests that difference embeddings suppress disease-specific (class-dependent) information while preserving relational structure, such as relative distances and angular relationships. As a result, these representations are inherently more suitable for cross-domain transfer, as they encode modalityagnostic structural knowledge rather than instance-level semantics. Empirical observations further demonstrate the effectiveness of the proposed framework. Comparative t-SNE visualizations (see Fig. 16) suggest that, without explicit domain alignment, conventional RKD exhibits limited alignment between teacher and student embedding distributions. In contrast, the proposed approach generates more aligned and coherent embeddings, facilitating more effective transfer of relational knowledge across modalities. In addition, the convergence curves (see Fig. 17) illustrate the training behavior of the proposed xReDA framework in terms of classification loss across voice, hand-drawing, and smartwatch modalities. The training trajectory of the proposed framework remains substantially stable, characterized by progressively reduced fluctuations and eventual convergence to a higher-performing solution. This stable behavior suggests effective learning of cross-domain alignment and distillation objectives during training. Overall, this formulation highlights that effective knowledge distillation in heterogeneous, unpaired settings can be achieved by shifting the focus from instance-level correspondence to structure-level consistency. By transferring the learned geometry of the embedding space, the proposed framework enables robust cross-domain generalization while preserving discriminative performance across diverse modalities.
5.2. Discussion on reliability
To further demonstrate the effectiveness of the proposed framework, Grad-CAM visualizations are incorporated to identify the input regions contributing most strongly to the model’s predictions. The heatmaps were generated from the layer immediately preceding the multimodal fusion stage [70,71], enabling visualization of modality-specific feature representations before multimodal aggregation. These visualizations provide an attention-based view of the regions and patterns that activate the modality-specific encoders and therefore offer qualitative insights into the learned representations. However, as with other interpretability approaches in medical AI, such visualizations should be interpreted as complementary explanatory evidence rather than definitive descriptions of the underlying decision-making process [72]. Since the encoders are optimized jointly through classification, RKD, and domaindiscrimination objectives, the highlighted regions may reflect the combined influence of these learning mechanisms. Furthermore, because the visualizations are generated prior to multimodal fusion, they provide only a partial view of the overall decision-making process. Fig. 18 presents representative Grad-CAM visualizations for the voice modality. The figure contains mel spectrogram inputs alongside their corresponding activation heatmaps for the MDVR-KCL dataset, illustrating the spectro-temporal regions that most strongly influence the model’s predictions. Distinct differences between HC and PD samples can be observed in the spectrogram patterns. HC samples exhibit relatively stable harmonic structures and consistent energy distributions across time and frequency, whereas PD samples show disrupted harmonic organization, increased temporal irregularity, and altered energy
