6. Ablation study
6.1. Selection of neuroimaging backbone
Table 5 presents a comparative analysis of multiple backbone architectures for the neuroimaging branch, highlighting the trade-off between classification performance and computational complexity in Parkinson’s Disease detection using DaTscan data. Although VGG16 achieves the highest accuracy (98.03%) with the adapter, this gain comes at the cost of substantially higher model size (136.40M parameters) and computational burden (40.06 GFLOPs), making it less practical for efficient deployment. In contrast, lighter architectures such as EfficientNetB0 offer significantly lower complexity but exhibit comparatively reduced performance. Among the evaluated models, DenseNet121 provides a well-balanced compromise, achieving competitive accuracy (96.50%) with a relatively small parameter count (9.06M) and moderate computational cost (7.26 GFLOPs). This favorable trade-off motivates its selection as the neuroimaging backbone in our framework.
6.2. Impact of the proposed modules on non-clinical networks
Table 6 presents the ablation results illustrating the individual and cumulative impact of each proposed module–(a) RDE, (b) TDE ( = FDE in the Fourier domain), (c) DFAB, and (d) xReDA–across Voice, Smartwatch, and Hand Drawing datasets. Starting from the baseline RDE, which extracts fundamental features from raw temporal data, the addition of TDE consistently improves performance in all modalities. The F1-score increases from 57.37% to 57.92% (+0.55%) in Voice, 80.81% to 84.96% (+4.15%) in Smartwatch, and 86.21% to 88.87% (+2.66%) in Hand-drawing. This demonstrates that incorporating transformed-domain (spectrogram-based) and wavelet-based, detail-enhanced representations complements rawdomain features by jointly capturing spectral and time-frequency dependencies, which are particularly beneficial for modeling physiological and behavioral signals with non-stationary dynamics.. Introducing the DFAB module further enhances model performance by adaptively emphasizing modality-relevant spatial and channel-level features. The F1-scores rise by 3.52%, 7.11%, and 5.65% for voice, smartwatch, and hand-drawing, respectively. This significant improvement confirms that DFAB effectively strengthens inter-feature interactions and fuses complementary information across domains, leading to more discriminative latent representations. Finally, integrating xReDA, the cross-modal relational domain adaptation module, yields the most pronounced gain–achieving 82.50%, 93.16%, and 96.22% F1-scores for the three modalities, respectively.
The improvements of 21.06%, 1.09%, and 1.70% over the previous configuration highlight xReDA’s capability to align latent feature distributions between heterogeneous modalities. By enforcing relational consistency across modalities, xReDA guides the non-clinical network to mimic the structural decision-making patterns of the neuroimaging network rather than merely matching feature values. This relational alignment helps the non-clinical models preserve clinically meaningful inter-sample relationships that are otherwise absent in low-cost sensing modalities. As a result, xReDA not only reduces the domain gap between clinical and non-clinical inputs but also equips the non-clinical network with richer, more discriminative representations, ultimately leading to stronger performance and improved generalization. Overall, each module contributes a distinct yet complementary role: TDE ( = FDE in the Fourier domain) enriches representational diversity, DFAB selectively fuses and emphasizes informative features, and xReDA bridges domain gaps through relational adaptation. Their progressive integration results in consistent performance gains across all multimodal datasets, validating the synergistic effectiveness of the proposed architecture.
7. Computational complexity analysis
As shown in Table 7, the computational complexity of the proposed method remains broadly comparable to existing baseline approaches across all three modalities, while delivering meaningful performance improvements. For the Voice modality, the proposed method achieves a test-time parameter count of 41.10M and a notably competitive latency of 14.92 ms, demonstrating that the added representational capacity of the proposed network does not translate into prohibitive inference overhead. Similarly, in the Smartwatch modality, the proposed method maintains a reasonable parameter footprint (36.92M at test time) and memory usage (147.70MB), remaining well within the same computational order of magnitude as prior works such as Varghese et al. and Hur et al. For Hand Drawing, while training-time FLOPs are moderately higher (42.56G), the test-time FLOPs reduce to 35.32G with the lowest latency of 8.71 ms across the group, underscoring the inference efficiency of the proposed design. Taken together, these results demonstrate that the proposed method achieves its performance gains without imposing a disproportionate computational burden, the complexity is commensurate with the state-of-the-art baselines, making it a practically viable and computationally responsible solution across diverse biometric modalities. Since the proposed framework is intended for accessible selfscreening using commonly available personal devices, we additionally evaluated its practical deployment feasibility across desktop, smartphone, and tablet client devices under a cloud-assisted inference setting. In this deployment configuration, data acquisition, lightweight preprocessing, and user interaction are performed locally on the client device, while computationally intensive inference is executed on a remote cloud server. For deployment evaluation, the trained models were hosted on a cloud-based CPU inference environment using the Hugging Face inference infrastructure, while the client devices communica