model is used as a supervisory reference, guiding networks trained on non-clinical modalities such as voice, smartwatch sensor data, and handdrawing patterns. DaTscan is employed as a reference source because it provides clinically established measurements of dopaminergic integrity, capturing disease-related patterns that are not directly observable in non-clinical modalities. To enable cross-modality knowledge transfer, a relational domain alignment strategy, termed xReDA, is proposed. Unlike conventional domain alignment methods, which typically enforce instance-level or feature-level matching between source and target domains, xReDA focuses on transferring relational information from the neuroimaging network to non-clinical networks. In this setting, featurelevel alignment alone may be limited in capturing the differences between clinically acquired imaging data and low-cost sensing signals, as these modalities exhibit substantially different data characteristics. Instead of relying on direct feature matching, xReDA encourages the nonclinical network to learn from the relational structure induced by the DaTscan-based model, preserving inter-sample relationships in a taskrelevant manner. By focusing on these relational patterns rather than individual feature values, xReDA provides a mechanism to better connect high-fidelity clinical data with non-clinical inputs, enabling the learning of more informative and discriminative representations that can improve generalization to unseen data. For each non-clinical modality, modality-specific architectures are employed to extract complementary information from both raw input and transformed-domain representations. Experimental results indicate that the proposed strategy consistently improves diagnostic performance and generalization compared to several unimodal and multimodal baselines. Moreover, when guided by DaTscan-derived knowledge, the xReDA-based framework shows notable gains in PD detection performance, suggesting its potential usefulness for reliable classification in practical settings. Overall, this study highlights an applicationoriented direction for cost-efficient, clinically informed AI-assisted PD diagnostics, with potential to support earlier identification of diseaserelated patterns under low-cost sensing scenarios.
